From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Do not close the pipe the server reads requests from.

No thread this process creates has ever worked. Every one reaches
"thread registered" in server_init_thread, hangs in its first server
call, and is killed:

    wineserver: protocol error 0028: read: Bad file descriptor
    wineserver: kill_thread 0028 exit=1 violent=1 from fatal_protocol_error+0xcf

EBADF, on the server's read of the new thread's request pipe. The client
closed it.

create_server_thread makes the pipe, hands the read end to the server
with wine_server_send_fd, names it in new_thread, and then closes it --
which is right where SCM_RIGHTS duplicated it into the receiver, because
the client's copy is then spent. Here nothing is duplicated: dup is EPERM
for every kind of descriptor on this console, so wine_server_send_fd
passes the descriptor's number and the in-process server, sharing this
descriptor table, reads that very fd. Closing it shuts the pipe the
server is about to read.

So the server reads EBADF on the first request the thread sends, calls it
a protocol error, and kills the thread. The thread never finishes
init_thread, never reaches wow64, never runs one guest instruction -- and
winmm, waiting on {ready event, thread handle} for a thread the server
has already executed, waits forever. That is GTA IV's black screen, and
it is why no second thread has ever appeared in a session log.

The same hazard is guarded twice already, with the reasoning spelled out
both times: server_init_thread's close of the reply pipe, and the server's
own close of this pipe's other end in create_thread. This was the third
and it was missed.

⚠ Closed only when the server refused the thread, where nobody owns the
descriptor. Otherwise it belongs to the server until the thread dies.
diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/thread.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/thread.c
index e5e20741c4..34421f9e84 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/thread.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/thread.c
@@ -1296,7 +1296,33 @@ static NTSTATUS create_server_thread( HANDLE *handle, struct thread_data **data_
             *handle = wine_server_ptr_handle( reply->handle );
             tid = reply->tid;
         }
+#ifdef __PROSPERO__
+        /* ⚠ Handed over, not duplicated. wine_server_send_fd passes this
+         * descriptor's number and nothing else -- a descriptor cannot be
+         * duplicated into the receiver here, dup is EPERM for every kind of
+         * descriptor on this console -- and the in-process server shares this
+         * descriptor table, so it reads this very fd. Closing it is closing
+         * the pipe the server reads the new thread's requests from, and the
+         * server's first read of it returns EBADF:
+         *
+         *     wineserver: protocol error 0028: read: Bad file descriptor
+         *     wineserver: kill_thread 0028 exit=1 violent=1
+         *                 from fatal_protocol_error+0xcf
+         *
+         * -- so every secondary thread this process has ever created was
+         * killed before it finished init_thread, which is why none has ever
+         * run a guest instruction, why no second thread has appeared in a log,
+         * and why GTA IV hangs forever on the winmm devices thread it is
+         * waiting for. The same hazard is already guarded in
+         * server_init_thread (the reply pipe) and in the server's own
+         * create_thread (the other end of this one); this was the third.
+         *
+         * Only close it when the server refused the thread, where nobody owns
+         * it; otherwise it belongs to the server until the thread dies. */
+        if (status) close( request_pipe[0] );
+#else
         close( request_pipe[0] );
+#endif
     }
     SERVER_END_REQ;
 
